selkies — desktop over WebRTC (Selkies, with synchronized audio)

Run a virtual desktop (Xorg dummy + XFCE) streamed to your browser via
WebRTC using Selkies-GStreamer, including audio from a PulseAudio sink:

    selkies-start      start the desktop + Selkies, prints the URL
    selkies-stop       tear it down

The desktop listens on port 8080 (HTTP) inside the sandbox. To reach
it from the host the port must be PUBLISHED when you start csb — it is
not exposed automatically:

    csb --addon selkies --publish 8080                 # dynamic host port
    csb --addon selkies --publish 127.0.0.1:8080:8080  # fixed host port

selkies-start prints the correct URL using the host port csb allocated
($CSB_PUBLISH_8080); without --publish it falls back to 8080.

No login is required: basic auth is disabled so the URL opens straight
to the desktop. This suits a localhost/published-port sandbox — anyone
who can reach the published port gets in, so do not bind it to a public
address. (Credentials cannot be embedded in the URL: the web client's
relative fetch() calls fail on URLs with embedded credentials.)

WebRTC media is relayed through a LOCAL coturn TURN server bundled in
the addon — no public STUN/TURN is used, and it works regardless of
network topology (Linux, a macOS VM, or a remote host). coturn listens
on TCP 3478 and is auto-published on 127.0.0.1:3478 (a fixed mapping, so
the same turn:127.0.0.1:3478 resolves from both the host browser and the
in-container streamer). All ICE is forced to relay through it
(iceTransportPolicy=relay). It is bound to 127.0.0.1 only; like the web
port, treat it as localhost-only and do not expose it publicly.

The TURN credentials default to selkies / csbturn; override before
selkies-start with CSB_SELKIES_TURN_USER and CSB_SELKIES_TURN_PASSWORD if
desired. Note these creds are NOT the security boundary — the localhost-only
publish of both 8080 and 3478 is. Don't expose either port publicly.

Audio and video are streamed together over WebRTC. Most browsers block
autoplaying audio with sound until you interact with the page — click
anywhere on the page or the speaker icon to start hearing audio.

The desktop resolution follows your browser window automatically: Selkies
resizes the remote display to match the window (rendering at native
resolution, not scaling the stream), up to a maximum of 5120x2880 (5K).
Set the INITIAL resolution (shown until a browser connects) before
selkies-start via CSB_SELKIES_GEOMETRY (default 1280x800x24; the depth
field is accepted but the desktop runs at 24-bit):

    CSB_SELKIES_GEOMETRY=1920x1080x24 selkies-start

HiDPI displays are handled automatically. On a Retina-class screen the
browser reports a devicePixelRatio above 1, and Selkies renders the
desktop at DEVICE pixels — a 1440x900 window becomes a 2880x1800 desktop
— so the picture stays sharp instead of being upscaled. The desktop DPI
is raised to match, so text keeps its physical size rather than shrinking
by half. That is why the resolution envelope is 5K: a 2x client doubles
whatever it asks for.

The browser reports its scale as soon as it connects, so this applies
from the first page load — you do not need to resize anything. Set the
INITIAL scale, used before any browser connects, with
CSB_SELKIES_DPI_SCALE (default 1; 2 means 192 DPI):

    CSB_SELKIES_DPI_SCALE=2 selkies-start

Only apps started AFTER a scale change pick up the new DPI. If you launch
an app from the shell before connecting a browser, and the browser then
reports a different scale, restart the app to pick up the new size.
